{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויהי לי שור וחמור", :translit "va-yhi li shor va-chamor", :gloss "and there was to me ox and donkey"} {:he "הקול קול יעקב", :translit "ha-kol kol ya'akov", :gloss "the voice is the voice of Jacob"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Allusion: ox → Joseph; donkey → Issachar"} {:kind :other, :note "Joseph identified as adversary of Esau per Obadiah 1:18"}], :frame {:text "Genesis 27:22 states \"the voice is the voice of Jacob,\" which rabbinic tradition reads as Jacob's power residing in Torah study. Obadiah 1:18 identifies the house of Joseph as a flame against the stubble of Esau, establishing Joseph as Esau's adversary. Genesis 49:14 compares Issachar to a strong donkey, and 1 Chronicles 12:32 states that the sons of Issachar possess understanding of the times, which rabbinic tradition (BT Megillah 14b) interprets as expertise in Torah law.", :sources ["Genesis 27:22" "Obadiah 1:18" "Genesis 49:14" "1 Chronicles 12:32" "BT Megillah 14b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לי שור", :translit "li shor", :gloss "to me ox"} {:he "שור", :translit "shor", :gloss "ox"} {:he "קרן יוסף", :translit "keren yosef", :gloss "horn of Joseph"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לי שור", :b "שנולד יוסף"} {:kind :gematria, :a "שור", :b "קרן יוסף"}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 33:17 blesses Joseph with 'his firstborn ox, and his horns are horns of a wild ox,' which rabbinic tradition identifies as the 'keren yosef,' the horn of Joseph, referring to the messianic descendant who will defeat Esau's descendants. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the ox in Jacob's message as encoding this messianic figure.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 33:17"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויהי לי שור", :translit "va-yhi li shor", :gloss "and there was to me ox"} {:he "ווי לי", :translit "oy li", :gloss "woe to me"} {:he "השור והחמור", :translit "ha-shor ve-ha-chamor", :gloss "the ox and the donkey"} {:he "הטמא והטהור", :translit "ha-tamei ve-ha-tahor", :gloss "the impure and the pure"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: ויהי לי read as ווי לי 'woe to me'"} {:kind :other, :note "Ox = pure species (kosher); donkey = impure species (non-kosher)"}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 11:3–4 distinguishes the ox, which chews the cud and has split hooves, as pure, from the donkey, which lacks both signs, as impure. Deuteronomy 22:10 prohibits plowing with an ox and donkey together. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the onset of ויהי as a cry of distress ('oy li') that pure and impure will be yoked together.", :sources ["Leviticus 11:3–4" "Deuteronomy 22:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Allusion: ox → Joseph; donkey → Issachar", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Joseph identified as adversary of Esau per Obadiah 1:18", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "לי שור", :a-value 546, :b "שנולד יוסף", :b-value 546, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :gematria, :a "שור", :a-value 506, :b "קרן יוסף", :b-value 506, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: ויהי לי read as ווי לי 'woe to me'", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Ox = pure species (kosher); donkey = impure species (non-kosher)", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:09:35.399880148Z", :model "glm-5.2"}